   "D133": {
    "topic": "Water",
    "text": "with D173, D186, D267, D268, D269, D285): Weather: players see how a map behaves through droughts and badtides before playing it. The editor's Drought and Badtide buttons are a day-by-day view: a click shows the hazard's worst (last) day at once with progress shown, never a frozen page; clicking again returns to the map's own water. A day strip on the water bar runs Day 0 (the map as it is) through the last day,…"
   },
   "D158": {
    "topic": "The editor and its tools",
    "text": "with D179, D184, D204): Live editing is how you edit a map (EDITOR_PLAN.md holds the vision): no plan-confirm-place flows, no Place button, no waiting. Responsive above all (every input answered in one or two frames; the display's frame rate while painting on 256²; no main-thread stalls; cancel and undo at once); direct manipulation; everything reversible; show, don't ask (live previews, limits and reasons while…"
   },
   "D182": {
    "topic": "The editor and its tools",
    "text": "with D184, D198, D247, D248, D263): The brush kit is the core of the editor: brushes feel like painting and the player can't mess up. All landform tools are eliminated (hill, plateau, ridge, canyon, valley, island, lake and resource-area objects, their handles; no presets). The top bar holds Raise, Lower, Flatten, Smooth, Naturalize, Select (a small button; M or Ctrl+drag also open it) and the forces; a row beneath…"
   },
   "D399": {
    "topic": "The editor and its tools",
    "text": "amends D387 (4), D368 (8)): Naturalize's land effect is a new algorithm, not tuning: today's per-tile rule (stepTile, a 20% per-tile hash and four-neighbour rules, nothing depending on Size) makes single-tile speckle, wrong in every capture and both looks. The target: edges wander but don't fray (one continuous line curving in and out by a few tiles, the curves' size following Size and their wander Strength); cliffs…"
   },
   "D404": {
    "topic": "The editor and its tools",
    "text": "with D368 (10), D399): Trees keep the build's rule through every edit, brushes included: the build marks a tree dead or alive from the moisture under it after each edit, so the saved file matches the ground. Naturalize adds and moves nothing; where its weathered ground becomes moist, the same tree comes back alive. Kyler, 2026-10-02."
   },
   "D425": {
    "topic": "The editor and its tools",
    "text": "supersedes D345 B5 for generated maps, with D368 (10), D404): No edit or force adds an object (#178): an edited map keeps only the objects the generation placed; water never adds or removes one, so a generated forest flooded by a lake stands in it dead, as the game does it, instead of being hidden and growing back; a broken slope stays gone; carried objects land where the force put them. A nightly sweep of every…"
   },
