 "perfect": [
  {
   "heading": "The whole experience",
   "text": "A whimsical, surprising terrain is put in front of me. I play with it: flatten the land to connect two rivers, craterize the whole area, add a few water sources. It looks interesting now, so let's play. From opening Dam Good Maps to playing a map I love takes minutes, and getting it into the game is one click, with no…",
   "points": []
  },
  {
   "heading": "Performance",
   "text": "Speed is part of perfect (PLAN §20 D380). Slowness in generation, the forces, batch jobs, analysis or the editor degrades the experience, so every feature meets its speed budget before it ships, and a speed regression blocks a merge like a failing test. Budgets are measured in a quiet window. For the renderer, moving…",
   "points": []
  },
  {
   "heading": "Maps",
   "text": "1. Every map feels designed by nature, not a program: no ruler-straight channels, stamped shapes or copied pieces. 2. Maps feel genuinely different from one another: across any handful, different openings, water stories and problems to solve, and no single type dominates. Only true near-duplicates are ever rejected;…",
   "points": [
    "Every map feels designed by nature, not a program: no ruler-straight channels, stamped shapes or copied pieces.",
    "Maps feel genuinely different from one another: across any handful, different openings, water stories and problems to solve, and no single type dominates. Only true near-duplicates are ever rejected;…",
    "Each map has a character: something about its land or water you could describe to a friend.",
    "The land invites building: interesting places to put a colony, not just a legal start.",
    "It plays exactly right in Timberborn, with no surprises when you load it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Challenge",
   "text": "The game's difficulty setting governs the game's difficulty. On the map, challenge comes from terrain. Timberborn becomes easy with plentiful trees, plentiful irrigated farmland, flat building space, and an easy place to dam water, so a harder map makes those hard to come by early, through interesting and unique…",
   "points": [
    "The floor, at every difficulty: enough logs reachable on foot from the start for the worst still-viable route to a Forester plus the first essentials (computed from the game's data, never below 120).…",
    "Hard slows expansion; it never starves the start: the start still survives its first cycles, and the pinch comes when the colony tries to grow.",
    "The puzzle pays off: a harder map rewards solving it (the big irrigable valley behind a gorge that's difficult to dam, the forest on a plateau worth the ramps). A harder map is a puzzle worth…"
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Water",
   "text": "1. What you see in Dam Good Maps is what you get in the game, every time. 2. You can read a map's water at a glance: where it comes from, where it goes, what happens in a drought. 3. Water has character: falls pour, rivers wind, badwater looks poisonous.",
   "points": [
    "What you see in Dam Good Maps is what you get in the game, every time.",
    "You can read a map's water at a glance: where it comes from, where it goes, what happens in a drought.",
    "Water has character: falls pour, rivers wind, badwater looks poisonous."
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "The editor",
   "text": "1. You never think about the tool, only the land. 2. Tools do what you meant; when they don't, one undo fixes it, and you understand why. 3. It has moments of delight, like watching a river carve through a mountain. 4. It never feels sluggish: small actions respond instantly, and big ones show something happening…",
   "points": [
    "You never think about the tool, only the land.",
    "Tools do what you meant; when they don't, one undo fixes it, and you understand why.",
    "It has moments of delight, like watching a river carve through a mountain.",
    "It never feels sluggish: small actions respond instantly, and big ones show something happening right away."
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Real places",
   "text": "\"The primary goal is to preserve the natural features and uniqueness of a real area: converting memorable landscapes into Timberborn worlds you recognise.\" (Kyler, D331)",
   "points": [
    "Someone who knows the place recognises it, and its signature feature is there.",
    "It's a great Timberborn map within a few edits (a water source, a moved start, some trees), not just a pretty copy.",
    "It's a canvas to reimagine: every tool and force works on it like any map, so a player can put a crater in Yosemite Valley and build a mega dam.",
    "The beauty of real places is the unique composition of terrain features. It's wonderful if it's playable, but not meeting the cutoff should not disqualify the place; the editor can fix shortcomings…"
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Playing it",
   "text": "What makes a generated map good to play (Kyler, feedback item 47; PLAN §20 D325, D331). Real places follow their own section; what they take from this one is listed there.",
   "points": [
    "Enough wood in the starting zone (the starting-logs floor).",
    "Berries near the start, enough for an Iron Teeth start.",
    "At least two mine sites, reachable.",
    "At least one badwater source, and fresh water the start can pump.",
    "Land built at the bottom: the deepest riverbed stands at least 3 levels above the map's floor, so there is room to dig and terraform early.",
    "Badwater running far across the map: it stays contained, never crosses the start's water or first farmland, and how much land it reaches is reported per theme.",
    "Stairs in the early game: the start's wood, berries, water and first farmland are reachable on foot, counting natural slopes.",
    "Heavy terraforming to get going: the start has enough level buildable land for its first buildings."
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "The look",
   "text": "1. It reads instantly as Timberborn, only cleaner. 2. Nothing looks flat, plastic or wrong next to the game.",
   "points": [
    "It reads instantly as Timberborn, only cleaner.",
    "Nothing looks flat, plastic or wrong next to the game."
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "The forces",
   "text": "Each passes the magic bar: it starts the instant you act; there's a \"wow\" within a couple of seconds; it's never the same twice; the result is land you'd want to build on; and nothing is scary (Esc or undo reverts it all instantly).",
   "points": []
  }
 ],
